  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00037` — **Oversized Raster**, `src/assets/hero-banner.png`, severity `low`, confidence `0.95`, effort `quick`.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)`\n> - `severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB`\n> - `format: png`\n\nSo: **253.9 KB (260,000 bytes)**, which clears the low threshold by ~27% and sits well under the 500 KB medium bar.\n\nOne thing the finding doesn't report is intrinsic dimensions, so I read them off the file: **1000 × 1000, 8-bit RGB, non-interlaced, no alpha channel.** That changes the recommendation materially — see below.\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\n254 KB is a `low` finding on a ladder that goes to 1 MB. Nothing is broken, no behavior is wrong, and blocking a landing-page PR over a quarter-megabyte would be disproportionate.\n\nBut I wouldn't wave it through either, for three reasons:\n\n1. It's the **hero** — almost certainly the LCP element. Of every byte in this PR, these are the ones that land directly on a Core Web Vitals number.\n2. The detector rates effort `quick`. A fix that's one commit and zero risk doesn't earn a follow-up ticket.\n3. The asset is **1000 × 1000 with no alpha**. A square image in a hero slot means most of those pixels get cropped or letterboxed away, and the absence of an alpha channel means PNG was never the right container. This isn't \"compress it a bit harder\" — the asset is the wrong shape in the wrong format.\n\nPractically: leave it as a required-before-merge change on the PR, not a merge block. If the team has a landing-page weight budget in CI, it becomes a hard block automatically and this conversation is moot.\n\n## The transformation\n\n**Dimensions.** Get the real number from the CSS — I don't have the hero's render box, and that box is what sets the target. The rule: never ship more than **2× the CSS width** of the render area; past 2× DPR the extra pixels are invisible on every shipping display. Assuming a conventional full-bleed hero capped at a ~1440px container:\n\n| Variant | Pixels | Covers | Byte target |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| mobile | 768w | ≤384 CSS px @2× | ≤40 KB |\n| tablet/desktop | 1280w | ≤640 CSS px @2× | ≤70 KB |\n| wide | 1920w | 960 CSS px @2×, 1280 @1.5× | ≤100 KB |\n\nCrop to the hero's aspect ratio **before** resizing — going from 1:1 to something like 3:1 discards two-thirds of the pixels for free, before any encoder runs. Total for all three AVIF variants should land near or under the current single PNG.\n\n**Format: AVIF primary, WebP fallback, both in a `<picture>`.**\n\nAVIF is the right primary here specifically because of what a hero is: large smooth gradients and photographic content. WebP's 8-bit 4:2:0 YUV pipeline bands visibly across a big gradient backdrop, and you end up raising quality to hide it, which eats the savings. AVIF holds gradients at low bitrate and runs ~20–30% smaller than WebP at matched perceptual quality on this content class. Support is universal on current browsers (Chrome/Edge 85+, Firefox 93+, Safari 16.4+).\n\nWebP earns the fallback slot rather than the PNG, because it covers Safari 14–16.3 at roughly a third of the PNG's bytes. Keeping the PNG as a third source would be dead weight — nothing that reaches it can't read WebP.\n\nI'd flip to **WebP-only** in three cases: the hero has crisp overlaid text, a logo, or a UI screenshot (AVIF smears fine high-contrast edges at aggressive quality settings, and near-lossless WebP is the safer tool); the build or CDN pipeline can only emit one derivative; or AVIF encode time in CI is a real cost — it's roughly an order of magnitude slower than `cwebp`.\n\n```bash\n# crop to the real hero aspect ratio first — 3:1 shown, adjust to the render box\nmagick hero-banner.png -gravity center -crop 1000x333+0+0 +repage -resize 1920x -strip hero-1920.png\n\navifenc -a end-usage=q -a cq-level=28 -a tune=ssim -j all hero-1920.png hero-1920.avif\ncwebp -q 80 -m 6 -sharp_yuv hero-1920.png -o hero-1920.webp\n```\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"hero-768.avif 768w, hero-1280.avif 1280w, hero-1920.avif 1920w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"hero-768.webp 768w, hero-1280.webp 1280w, hero-1920.webp 1920w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <img src=\"hero-1280.webp\" width=\"1920\" height=\"640\" alt=\"…\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\">\n</picture>\n```\n\n`fetchpriority=\"high\"` and **no** `loading=\"lazy\"` — lazy-loading the LCP image is a net regression. The explicit `width`/`height` reserve layout space and keep this out of CLS.\n\n## Two notes on scope\n\nThis is a whole-repo scan (39 findings), not a diff, so confirm these are actually yours before acting: `crime_00035` — `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`, **high** severity, two base64 rasters (`image/jpeg`, `image/png`) embedded in a 592-byte SVG — and `crime_00038` — `src/assets/check-icon.png`, a 32×32 PNG that wants to be an SVG. Same directory, same class of problem, `quick`/`small` effort. If they're in this PR, they belong in the same asset-cleanup commit; the embedded-raster SVG is the higher-severity of the three by some distance.",
